            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book presents a highly coherent, multi-faceted argument that the biblical 'new moon' (ḥodesh) is the full moon, rather than the dark conjunction or the waxing crescent. The traditional crescent/dark moon interpretation struggles with several scriptural details: the east gate opening for new moon worship (Ezekiel 46:1-3) since a crescent rises in the west at sunset; the poetic equation of ḥodesh and keseh (full moon) in Psalm 81:3; the predictability of the day in 1 Samuel 20; the pitch darkness of the Abrahamic covenant on the 15th of the month (Genesis 15); and the mechanics of the midday darkness at the crucifixion on Passover (the 14th/15th), since a solar eclipse is impossible during a mid-month full moon. The book's core identification resolves all these textual anomalies elegantly, making it the best explanation of the supplied scriptural evidence.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The biblical 'new moon' (ḥodesh) is the full moon, which marks the start of the scriptural month.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The 'renewed moon' translates the root meaning of complete restoration (hadash).\",\n    \"It is the phase that marks the start of the month.\",\n    \"It stands in the east gate.\",\n    \"It is the sign of the appointed times.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The application of letter-by-letter pictographic meanings to 'hodesh' is an etymological method many linguists reject as anachronistic for biblical Hebrew.\",\n    \"The connection between the 12 gates of Revelation 21, the pearls, and the 12 constellations of the Mazzaroth is an intricate typological synthesis that relies on heavily interpretive links.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The 'new moon' refers to the dark astronomical conjunction or the first visible waxing crescent, which historically governed the calendars of ancient Near Eastern nations and post-exilic Rabbinic Judaism.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The new moon is the dark conjunction or first visible crescent.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter-interpretation denies that the full moon initiates the month, asserting instead that the month begins with the moon's darkest or thinnest visible phase.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Ancient Near Eastern calendars (like Babylon and Egypt) and Rabbinic Judaism historically started their months with the dark moon or the sighting of the first crescent.\",\n    \"The concept of 'new' intuitively aligns with a light emerging from darkness (the sliver) rather than starting from peak fullness.\",\n    \"The three hours of darkness at the crucifixion can be attributed to an entirely supernatural event without needing to align with the natural mechanism of a solar eclipse.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Explains Ezekiel 46:1-3, where the east gate is opened for new moon worship, perfectly matching the full moon rising in the east at sunset.\",\n    \"Explains the direct poetic parallelism in Psalm 81:3 without needing to insert an absent 'and' between the renewed moon and the full moon.\",\n    \"Explains how David knew with certainty 'tomorrow is the new moon' in 1 Samuel 20, as the full moon's arrival is precisely predictable by observing moonset, unlike the weather-dependent crescent sighting.\",\n    \"Solves the astronomical impossibility of a solar eclipse on Passover; if the month starts on the full moon, Passover (mid-month) falls on the dark moon, allowing the natural mechanism of an eclipse to be supernaturally intensified.\",\n    \"Explains the 'great darkness' of Abraham's covenant on the 15th of the month (Genesis 15), which would be impossible if the 15th were a bright full moon.\",\n    \"Aligns with the root meaning of 'hadash' as a complete restoration or renewal to perfection, rather than a partial sliver.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Aligns with the documented historical calendars of surrounding ancient cultures and later Jewish tradition.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Ezekiel 46:1-3\",\n    \"Psalms 81:3\",\n    \"Amos 8:9\",\n    \"Matthew 27:45\",\n    \"Genesis 15:12-17\",\n    \"1 Samuel 20:5\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
